TS Phone: (864) 295-3492 Greenville, SC [REDACTED] specialized diagnostic services Fax: (864) 455-8926 IHC Prognostic Report Patient Name DOB Specimen ID Account CSI Case Number Weaver, Rachel 03/24/1977 M25-35797-A1 C00499 WHC25-012873 BIOMARKER RESULTS ER (SP1) Negative (&lt; 1%) Low Positive (1% - 10%) Positive (&gt; 10% - 100%) PR (1E2) Negative (&lt; 1%) Positive (1% - 100%) Ki-67 (MIB-1) Low Proliferation Rate (&lt; 10%) Intermediate Proliferation Rate (10% - 20%) High Proliferation Rate (&gt; 20% - 100%) p53 (9p53-11) Negative (&lt; 35%) Positive (&gt; 35% - 100%) If the initial ER result is &lt; 10% or the ER intensity is weak (1+), then internal control tissues are re-reviewed following ASCO/CAP 2020 Guidelines. The "Low Positive" ER designation applies only to invasive carcinoma, and does not apply to PR expression or DCIS. The American Society of Clinical Oncology and College of American Pathology (1,2): The cancer in this sample has a suspect ER (1-10%) of ER expression by IHC. There are limited data on the overall benefit of endocrine therapies for patients with low level (1-10%) ER expression. However, but studies suggest results may be considered as potentially eligible for endocrine therapy. There are also data from retrospective studies suggesting that low ER positive tumors (1-10% of invasive cells) may still respond to ER signaling. Based on the latest ASCO/CAP 2020 ER/PR Guidelines, ER testing should also be performed on DCIS tumors without invasion since there is growing clinical literature that adjuvant endocrine therapy (tamoxifen) significantly decreases the risk of local and systemic tumor invasion, and/or future invasive cancer in these patients. Although only ER should be used to select these patients for endocrine therapy, reporting PR results may still provide prognostic information and is considered optional in DCIS patients. With selected invasive breast cancer subtypes, ER results are usually histologically concordant with these tumors. Thus, when tumor histology is concordant with ER results (&lt; 10%, staining intensity (1+), and/or is reported as "ER-POSITIVE." But if the tumor histology is not concordant with these results, ER should be repeated. Staining intensity (1+) OR any other ER result are classified in relation to tumor histology, invasive lobular carcinoma (ILC), or is cytoplasm is staining instead of nuclear, the pathologist must correlate with Histochemical findings on the Primary invasive carcinoma: (1) Discordant tumors that are aberrantly ER NEGATIVE. For example, LG invasive ductal carcinoma of no special type (Lobular Carcinoma Type, Apocrine differentiations), and (2) Discordant tumors that are aberrantly ER POSITIVE. For example, high grade ductal carcinomas or [?] Invasive papillary carcinomas]. In a recent study (Neunier, 2023), an optimal p53 Index cutoff of &gt; 35% was utilized to help</data>
